{"timestamp":"2026-08-20T17:11:45.289Z","ordinal":1114,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a02028-4589-7cd3-a1f3-fae663db6267","call_id":"call_2b8A8aTbIUb81RxBuEodHP28","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 2.0 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"\"Extend Claude with skills - Claude Code Docs (https://code.claude.com/docs/en/slash-commands)\\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn3search1\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 1090\\nL0: > ## Documentation Index\\nL1: >\\nL2: > Fetch the complete documentation index at: cite0†/docs/llms.txt L3: >\\nL4: > Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\\nL5: \\nL6: cite1†Skip to main content L7: \\nL8: cite2†Claude Code Docs home page L9: \\nL10: Search...\\nL11: \\nL12: Navigation\\nL13: \\nL14: Skills\\nL15: \\nL16: Extend Claude with skills\\nL17: \\nL18: cite2†Getting started cite3†Build with Claude Code cite4†Administration cite5†Configuration cite6†Reference cite7†Agent SDK cite8†What's New cite9†Resources L276: cite86†Cowork†claude.com sessions and cite87†cloud sessions , including cite88†routines , don’t read `~/.claude/skills/` on your machine. Both interactive and scheduled Cowork sessions load the skills enabled for your claude.ai account, synced at session start; manage them from Customize in the Desktop app sidebar or from the skills settings on claude.ai. Cloud sessions additionally load project skills committed to the cloned repository’s `.claude/skills/`.\\nL278:   * For Cowork and cloud sessions, enable the skill for your claude.ai account.\\nL279:   * For cloud sessions, you can instead commit the skill to the repository’s `.claude/skills/`, or ship it in a plugin declared in the repository’s `.claude/settings.json`. Repo-declared plugins cite87†install at session start ; plugins enabled only in your user settings don’t transfer.\\nL286: This section applies to you if you enabled skills for your claude.ai account. In Cowork and cloud sessions, Claude Code loads those skills without any setup on your machine. In any other session on your machine, Claude Code loads them only after you turn syncing on with cite81†`CLAUDE_CODE_SYNC_SKILLS` in a non-interactive run, as cite43†Where synced skills load describes.\\nL288: ####\\nL289: \\nL290: cite43†​ L291: \\nL292: Where synced skills load\\nL293: \\nL294: In a Cowork or cloud session, Claude Code loads the skills enabled for your claude.ai account, and cite41†Skills in Cowork and cloud sessions says how to choose which skills those sessions get. In any other session on your machine, Claude Code loads them only after you download them once in a non-interactive run:\\nL295: \\nL296: 1\\nL297: \\nL298: Enable the skills for your claude.ai account\\nL299: Enable each skill you want for your claude.ai account, as cite41†Skills in Cowork and cloud sessions describes. Claude Code downloads only the skills you enabled, and it needs your claude.ai sign-in to download them.\\nL300: \\nL301: 2\\nL302: \\nL303: Run Claude Code in non-interactive mode with syncing turned on\\nL304: Claude Code downloads synced skills only when you run it in cite26†non-interactive mode with the `-p` flag and set cite81†`CLAUDE_CODE_SYNC_SKILLS` to `1`. The prompt you pass doesn’t affect the download.\\nL305: \\nL306:     `CLAUDE_CODE_SYNC_SKILLS=1 claude -p \\\"List the skills you have available\\\"\\nL307:     `\\nL308: Claude Code downloads the skills into `~/.claude/skills/synced/`, answers the prompt, and exits like any other non-interactive run. The downloaded skills stay on disk after it exits, so you don’t need to keep the run open. Claude Code downloads skills only during a run with `CLAUDE_CODE_SYNC_SKILLS` set, so after you enable or change a skill on claude.ai, run the command again. To change how long the run waits for the sync before it answers the prompt, set cite81†`CLAUDE_CODE_SYNC_SKILLS_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS` .\\nL319: Claude Code skips a synced skill whose name matches any other command, and that other command runs. The other command can be a built-in command, a cite33†bundled skill , a skill at any cite37†local level , a plugin skill, a file in `.claude/commands/`, or an cite90†MCP prompt . Claude Code also reserves the names of its own built-in commands and bundled skills even when they’re unavailable in your session, for example after you turn bundled skills off, so it skips a synced skill with one of those names too.\\nL320: Claude Code labels synced skills so you can tell where they came from. The `/skills` menu and `/context` group synced skills under `claude.ai sync`, and the `/` command menu marks them as coming from claude.ai. When it compares names, Claude Code ignores case, spacing, and invisible characters, and treats compatibility forms such as fullwidth letters and dash variants as their plain equivalents, so a synced `Commit` can’t load beside a local `commit`.\\nL322: ####\\nL323: \\nL324: cite45†​ L325: \\nL326: How Claude Code handles the frontmatter of a synced skill\\nL327: \\nL328: Claude Code applies two rules to a synced skill’s frontmatter:\\nL329:   * Claude Code honors the frontmatter in every kind of session, so an `allowed-tools` grant goes through the normal cite91†permission flow .\\nL330:   * Claude Code sanitizes the display text the skill supplies, such as its description. It removes control characters, and in text that reaches Claude, such as the description, it also escapes angle brackets so the text can’t imitate Claude Code’s internal formatting.\\nL331: ####\\nL332: \\nL333: cite46†​ L334: \\nL335: How Claude Code handles the body of a synced skill\\nL336: \\nL337: What Claude Code does with a synced skill’s body depends on where the session runs:\\nL338:   * In a cloud session, the body keeps the behavior a local skill has, because the session runs in an isolated container.\\nL339:   * In a Cowork session on your desktop, the body keeps the behavior a local skill has, except that Claude Code replaces every `!` command line with the cite60†`disableSkillShellExecution` placeholder , as it does for every skill you supply there.\\nL340:   * In any other session on your machine, Claude Code doesn’t run cite60†`!` commands , doesn’t attach the files that `@` references name the way it does for a local skill, and doesn’t substitute the `${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}` and `${CLAUDE_SESSION_ID}` placeholders, so the `@` references and both placeholders reach Claude as literal text. A `!` command line reaches Claude as literal text too, or as that placeholder when `disableSkillShellExecution` is on.\\nL348:   * Personal or project skill: delete the skill’s directory, `~/.claude/skills/<skill-name>/` or `.claude/skills/<skill-name>/`. Claude Code cite38†drops it from `/skills` in the current session ; content from an invocation earlier in the session cite56†stays in context until the session ends.\\nL349:   * Enterprise skill: an administrator deletes the skill’s directory from `.claude/skills/` inside the cite80†managed settings directory , for example `/etc/claude-code/.claude/skills/<skill-name>/` on Linux.\\nL350:   * Plugin skill: disable or uninstall the plugin that provides it, from the `/plugin` menu or with `/plugin uninstall <plugin-name>@<marketplace-name>`. Claude Code unloads the plugin’s skills after you run `/reload-plugins` or restart; see cite92†Apply plugin changes without restarting .\\nL351:   * Skill synced from claude.ai: turn the skill off for your claude.ai account, in the same place you cite41†enabled it . Claude Code removes it from `~/.claude/skills/synced/` the next time it cite43†syncs your skills . If you delete the directory by hand instead, the next sync downloads it again while the skill stays enabled on claude.ai.\\nL570: When you or Claude invoke a skill, the rendered `SKILL.md` content enters the conversation as a single message and stays there for the rest of the session. This persistence applies to the skill’s instructions, not its permissions: an cite57†`allowed-tools` grant clears when you send your next message. Claude Code does not re-read the skill file on later turns, so write guidance that should apply throughout a task as standing instructions rather than one-time steps.\\nL571: When Claude re-invokes a skill whose rendered content is identical to the copy already in context, Claude Code adds a short note that the skill is already loaded rather than a second copy of the content. When the rendered content differs, because the arguments changed or a cite60†dynamic context command produced new output, Claude Code appends the full content again. cite108†Auto-compaction carries invoked skills forward within a token budget.\\nL572: When the conversation is summarized to free context, Claude Code re-attaches the most recent invocation of each skill after the summary, keeping the first 5,000 tokens of each. Re-attached skills share a combined budget of 25,000 tokens. Claude Code fills this budget starting from the most recently invoked skill, so older skills can be dropped entirely after compaction if you have invoked many in one session.\\nL573: If a skill seems to stop influencing behavior after the first response, the content is usually still present and the model is choosing other tools or approaches. Strengthen the skill’s `description` and instructions so the model keeps preferring it, or use cite109†hooks to enforce behavior deterministically. If the skill is large or you invoked several others after it, re-invoke it after compaction to restore the full content.\\nL574: ###\\nL575: \\nL576: cite57†​ L577: \\nL578: Pre-approve tools for a skill\\nL579: The `allowed-tools` field grants permission for the listed tools during the turn that invokes the skill, so Claude can use them without prompting you for approval. The grant clears when you send your next message, even though the skill content cite56†stays in context ; invoking the skill again re-applies it for that turn. It does not restrict which tools are available: every tool remains callable, and your cite91†permission settings still govern tools that are not listed.\\nL580: To pre-approve tools for the whole session rather than a single turn, add allow rules to those permission settings instead. Workspace trust doesn’t gate this field. Claude Code applies a project skill’s `allowed-tools` whenever you or Claude invoke the skill, including in a `-p` run in a folder you’ve never trusted. A skill can grant itself broad tool access, so review the `allowed-tools` of skills checked into a repository before you run Claude Code there.\\nL581: This skill lets Claude run git commands without per-use approval whenever you invoke it:\\nL582: \\nL583:     `---\\nL584:     name: commit\\nL585:     description: Stage and commit the current changes\\nL586:     disable-model-invocation: true\\nL587:     allowed-tools: Bash(git add *) Bash(git commit *) Bash(git status *)\\nL588:     ---\\nL589:     `\\nL590: To remove tools from Claude’s available pool while a skill is active, list them in `disallowed-tools` in the skill’s frontmatter. The restriction clears when you send your next message. Like deny rules, the field can’t remove cite95†`EndConversation` while any other tool remains. To block tools across all skills and prompts, add deny rules in your cite91†permission settings .\\nL591: ###\\nL592: \\nL593: cite58†​ L594: \\nL595: Pass arguments to skills\\nL596: Both you and Claude can pass arguments when invoking a skill. Arguments are available via the `$ARGUMENTS` placeholder. This skill fixes a GitHub issue by number. The `$ARGUMENTS` placeholder gets replaced with whatever follows the skill name:\\nL597: \\nL598:     `---\\nL599:     name: fix-issue\\nL600:     description: Fix a GitHub issue\\nL601:     disable-model-invocation: true\\nL602:     ---\\nL603: \\nL604:     Fix GitHub issue $ARGUMENTS following our coding standards.\\nL605: \\nL606:     1. Read the issue description\\nL607:     2. Understand the requirements\\nL612: When you run `/fix-issue 123`, Claude receives “Fix GitHub issue 123 following our coding standards…” If you invoke a skill with arguments but the skill doesn’t include `$ARGUMENTS`, Claude Code appends `ARGUMENTS: <your input>` to the end of the skill content so Claude still sees what you typed. You can also stack several skills at the start of one message. Typing `/write-tests /fix-issue 123` loads both skills and passes the trailing text `123` as `$ARGUMENTS` to each of them.\\nL613: Before v2.1.199, only the first skill loaded and received `/fix-issue 123` as literal argument text. Claude Code expands the first skill plus up to five more stacked after it. Expansion stops at the first token that isn’t an inline user-invocable skill, so a skill that runs as a cite63†forked subagent , such as cite110†`/code-review` , or one whose arguments may themselves start with a slash command, such as `/loop`, also ends the run there.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nExtend Claude Code - Claude Code Docs (https://code.claude.com/docs/en/features-overview)\\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn3search2\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 366\\nL133: The same triggers tell you when to update what you already have. A repeated mistake or a recurring review comment is a CLAUDE.md edit, not a one-off correction in chat. A workflow you keep tweaking by hand is a skill that needs another revision.\\nL134: ###\\nL135: \\nL136: cite35†​ L137: \\nL138: Compare similar features\\nL139: \\nL140: Some features can seem similar. For a deeper walkthrough of choosing between them, see cite54†Steering Claude Code: when to use CLAUDE.md, skills, hooks, and subagents†claude.com on the blog. Here’s how to tell them apart.\\nL141: \\nL142:   * Skill vs Subagent\\nL143: \\nL144:   * CLAUDE.md vs Skill\\nL145: \\nL146:   * CLAUDE.md vs Rules vs Skills\\nL147: \\nL148:   * Subagent vs Agent team\\nL149: \\nL150:   * MCP vs Skill\\nL151: \\nL152:   * Hook vs Skill\\nL153: \\nL154: Skills and subagents solve different problems:\\nL157: Aspect  | Skill  | Subagent\\nL158: --- | --- | ---\\nL159: What it is  | Reusable instructions, knowledge, or workflows  | Isolated worker with its own context\\nL160: Key benefit  | Share content across contexts  | Context isolation. Work happens separately, only summary returns\\nL161: cite15†Context window impact  | Adds to your main window  | Uses a separate window with its own input and output tokens\\nL162: Best for  | Reference material, invocable workflows  | Tasks that read many files, parallel work, specialized workers\\nL163: Skills can be reference or action. Reference skills provide knowledge Claude uses throughout your session (like your API style guide). Action skills tell Claude to do something specific (like `/deploy` that runs your deployment workflow).Use a subagent when you need context isolation or when your context window is getting full. The subagent might read dozens of files or run extensive searches, but your main conversation only receives a summary.\\nL164: Since subagent work doesn’t consume your main context, this is also useful when you don’t need the intermediate work to remain visible. Custom subagents can have their own instructions and can preload skills.They can combine. A subagent can preload specific skills (`skills:` field). A skill can run in isolated context using `context: fork`. See cite43†Skills for details.\\nL165: Both store instructions, but they load differently and serve different purposes.\\nL166: \\nL167: Aspect  | CLAUDE.md  | Skill\\nL168: --- | --- | ---\\nL169: Loads  | Every session, automatically  | On demand\\nL170: Can include files  | Yes, with `@path` imports  | Yes, with `@path` imports\\nL171: Can trigger workflows  | No  | Yes, with `/<name>`\\nL172: Best for  | ”Always do X” rules  | Reference material, invocable workflows\\nL173: Put it in CLAUDE.md if Claude should always know it: coding conventions, build commands, project structure, “never do X” rules.Put it in a skill if it’s reference material Claude needs sometimes (API docs, style guides) or a workflow you trigger with `/<name>` (deploy, review, release).Rule of thumb: Keep CLAUDE.md under 200 lines. If it’s growing, move reference content to skills or split into cite55†`.claude/rules/` files.\\nL174: \\nL175: All three store instructions, but they load differently:\\nL176: Aspect  | CLAUDE.md  | `.claude/rules/`  | Skill\\nL177: --- | --- | --- | ---\\nL178: Loads  | Every session  | Every session, or when matching files are opened  | On demand, when invoked or relevant\\nL179: Scope  | Whole project  | Can be scoped to file paths  | Task-specific\\nL180: Best for  | Core conventions and build commands  | Language-specific or directory-specific guidelines  | Reference material, repeatable workflows\\nL181: Use CLAUDE.md for instructions every session needs: build commands, test conventions, project architecture.Use rules to keep CLAUDE.md focused. Rules with cite56†`paths` frontmatter only load when Claude works with matching files, saving context.Use skills for content Claude only needs sometimes, like API documentation or a deployment checklist you trigger with `/<name>`.\\nL182: \\nL183: Both parallelize work, but they’re architecturally different:\\nL184:   * Subagents run inside your session and report results back to your main context\\nL185:   * Agent teams are independent Claude Code sessions that communicate with each other\\nL186: Aspect  | Subagent  | Agent team\\nL187: --- | --- | ---\\nL188: Context  | Own context window; results return to the caller  | Own context window; fully independent\\nL189: Communication  | Reports results back to the main agent only  | Teammates message each other directly\\nL190: Coordination  | Main agent manages all work  | Self-coordination through messages, plus a shared task list for cite48†agents that have the Task tools L191: Best for  | Focused tasks where only the result matters  | Complex work requiring discussion and collaboration\\nL192: Token cost  | Lower: results summarized back to main context  | Higher: each teammate is a separate Claude instance\\nL193: Use a subagent when you need a quick, focused worker: research a question, verify a claim, review a file. The subagent does the work and returns a summary. Your main conversation stays clean.Use an agent team when teammates need to share findings, challenge each other, and coordinate independently.\\nL194: Agent teams are best for research with competing hypotheses, parallel code review, and new feature development where each teammate owns a separate piece.Transition point: If you’re running parallel subagents but hitting context limits, or if your subagents need to communicate with each other, agent teams are the natural next step. For separate sessions that pass messages to each other without a team, see cite57†cross-session messaging .\\nL195: Agent teams are experimental and disabled by default. See cite47†agent teams for setup and current limitations.\\nL196: \\nL197: MCP connects Claude to external services. Skills extend what Claude knows, including how to use those services effectively.\\nL198: Aspect  | MCP  | Skill\\nL199: --- | --- | ---\\nL200: What it is  | Protocol for connecting to external services  | Knowledge, workflows, and reference material\\nL201: Provides  | Tools and data access  | Knowledge, workflows, reference material\\nL202: Examples  | Slack integration, database queries, browser control  | Code review checklist, deploy workflow, API style guide\\nL203: These solve different problems and work well together:MCP gives Claude purpose-built tools for an external system, with the connection and authentication handled by the server.Skills give Claude knowledge about how to use those tools effectively, plus workflows you can trigger with `/<name>`. A skill might include your team’s database schema and query patterns, or a `/post-to-slack` workflow with your team’s message formatting rules.\\nL204: Claude Code runs a hook at a lifecycle event; it loads a skill into context for Claude to apply.\\nL205: Aspect  | Hook  | Skill\\nL206: --- | --- | ---\\nL207: Runs  | A shell command, HTTP request, LLM prompt, or subagent  | Instructions Claude reads and follows\\nL208: Triggered by  | cite58†Lifecycle events such as `PostToolUse` or `SessionStart`  | You typing `/<name>`, or Claude matching the description to your task\\nL209: Determinism  | Always fires on its event; the trigger is guaranteed  | Claude interprets the instructions; outcome can vary\\nL210: Context cost  | Zero unless the hook returns output  | Description loads each session; full content loads when used\\nL211: Best for  | Linting after edits, blocking unsafe commands, logging, notifications  | Workflows that need reasoning, reference material, multi-step tasks\\nL212: Use a hook when the action must happen the same way every time and doesn’t need Claude to think. For example: format on save, reject `rm -rf /`, post a Slack message when a session ends.Use a skill when Claude should decide how to apply the steps, or when the content is knowledge rather than a script. For example: a `/release` checklist, your API style guide, a debugging playbook.Put guardrails in hooks. An instruction like “never edit `.env`” in CLAUDE.md or a skill is a request, not a guarantee.\\nL213: A `PreToolUse` hook that blocks the edit is enforcement. If a rule must hold every time, make it a hook rather than a prompt instruction.Hook output lands in context. A `PostToolUse` hook that runs your linter feeds results back as text Claude reads; a `/fix-lint` skill tells Claude how to resolve them.\\nL214: ###\\nL215: \\nL216: cite36†​ L217: \\nL218: Understand how features layer\\nL219: \\nL220: Features can be defined at multiple levels: user-wide, per-project, via plugins, or through managed policies. You can also nest CLAUDE.md files in subdirectories or place skills in specific packages of a monorepo. When the same feature exists at multiple levels, here’s how they layer:\\nL221:   * CLAUDE.md files are additive: all levels contribute content to Claude’s context simultaneously. Files from your working directory and above load at launch; subdirectories load as you work in them. When instructions conflict, Claude uses judgment to reconcile them, with more specific instructions typically taking precedence. See cite59†how CLAUDE.md files load .\\nL230: Each extension solves a different problem: CLAUDE.md handles always-on context, skills handle on-demand knowledge and workflows, MCP handles external connections, subagents handle isolation, and hooks handle automation. Real setups combine them based on your workflow. For example, you might use CLAUDE.md for project conventions, a skill for your deployment workflow, MCP to connect to your database, and a hook to run linting after every edit. Each feature handles what it’s best at.\\nL282: Skills are extra capabilities in Claude’s toolkit. They can be reference material (like an API style guide) or invocable workflows you trigger with `/<name>` (like `/deploy`). Claude Code includes cite68†bundled skills like `/code-review`, `/batch`, and `/debug` that work out of the box. You can also create your own. Claude uses skills when appropriate, or you can invoke one directly.When: Depends on the skill’s configuration. By default, descriptions load at session start and full content loads when used.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nExplore the context window - Claude Code Docs (https://code.claude.com/docs/en/context-window)\\nciteturn4view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn3search9\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 152\\nL0: > ## Documentation Index\\nL1: >\\nL2: > Fetch the complete documentation index at: cite0†/docs/llms.txt L3: >\\nL4: > Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\\nL5: \\nL6: cite1†Skip to main content L7: \\nL8: cite2†Claude Code Docs home page L9: \\nL10: Search...\\nL11: \\nL12: Navigation\\nL13: \\nL14: Core concepts\\nL15: \\nL16: Explore the context window\\nL17: \\nL18: cite2†Getting started cite3†Build with Claude Code cite4†Administration cite5†Configuration cite6†Reference cite7†Agent SDK cite8†What's New cite9†Resources L19: \\nL20: ### Getting started\\nL21: \\nL22:   * cite2†Overview L23:   * cite10†Quickstart L24:   * cite11†Changelog L25: ### Core concepts\\nL26: \\nL27:   * cite12†How Claude Code works L28:   * cite13†Extend Claude Code L29:   * cite14†Explore the .claude directory L30:   * cite15†Explore the context window L31:   * cite16†Prompt caching L32: \\nL33: ### Use Claude Code\\nL34: \\nL35:   * cite17†Store instructions and memories L36:   * cite18†Permission modes L37:   * cite19†Manage sessions L38:   * cite20†Common workflows L39:   * cite21†Prompt library L40:   * cite22†Best practices L41: ### Platforms and integrations\\nL42: \\nL43:   * cite23†Overview L44:   * cite24†Remote Control L45:   * Claude Code on the web\\nL46: \\nL47:   * Claude Code on desktop\\nL48: \\nL49:   * cite25†Mobile L50:   * cite26†Chrome extension L51:   * cite27†Computer use (preview) L52:   * cite28†Visual Studio Code L53:   * cite29†JetBrains IDEs L54:   * Code review & CI/CD\\nL55: \\nL56:   * cite30†Claude Code in Slack L57:   * cite31†Claude Tag L58: \\nL59: Core concepts\\nL60: # Explore the context window\\nL61: \\nL62: An interactive simulation of how Claude Code’s context window fills during a session. See what loads automatically, what each file read costs, and when rules and hooks fire.\\nL63: \\nL64: Copy page Copy page\\nL65: Claude Code’s context window holds everything Claude knows about your session: your instructions, the files it reads, its own responses, and content that never appears in your terminal. The timeline below plays a full session from startup to compaction: what loads before you type, what each file read, rule, and hook adds as Claude works, and how a subagent keeps large reads out of your context. See cite32†the written breakdown for the same content as a list.\\nL66: ##\\nL67: \\nL68: cite32†​ L69: \\nL70: What the timeline shows\\nL71: \\nL72: The session walks through a realistic flow with representative token counts:\\nL73:   * Before you type anything: CLAUDE.md, auto memory, MCP tool names, and skill descriptions all load into context. Your own setup may add more here, like an cite33†output style or text from cite6†`--append-system-prompt` , which both go into the system prompt the same way.\\nL74:   * As Claude works: each file read adds to context, cite34†path-scoped rules load automatically alongside matching files, and a cite35†PostToolUse hook fires after each edit.\\nL75:   * The follow-up prompt: a cite36†subagent handles the research in its own separate context window, so the large file reads stay out of yours. Only the summary and a small metadata trailer come back.\\nL76:   * At the end: `/compact` replaces the conversation with a structured summary. Most startup content reloads automatically; the table below shows what happens to each mechanism.\\nL77: ##\\nL78: \\nL79: cite37†​ L80: \\nL81: What survives compaction\\nL82: \\nL83: When a long session compacts, Claude Code summarizes the conversation history to fit the context window. As of v2.1.198, the summarization request inherits your session’s cite38†extended thinking configuration, so it reasons with thinking enabled when your session has it enabled and stays off otherwise. Thinking affects only how the summary is produced; your session settings are unchanged afterward. What happens to your instructions depends on how they were loaded:\\nL84: Mechanism  | After compaction\\nL85: --- | ---\\nL86: System prompt and output style  | Unchanged; not part of message history\\nL87: Project-root CLAUDE.md and unscoped rules  | Re-injected from disk\\nL88: Auto memory  | Re-injected from disk\\nL89: Rules with `paths:` frontmatter  | Lost until a matching file is read again\\nL90: Nested CLAUDE.md in subdirectories  | Lost until a file in that subdirectory is read again\\nL91: Invoked skill bodies  | Re-injected, capped at 5,000 tokens per skill and 25,000 tokens total; oldest dropped first\\nL92: Hooks  | Not applicable; hooks run as code, not context\\nL93: Path-scoped rules and nested CLAUDE.md files load into message history when their trigger file is read, so compaction summarizes them away with everything else. They reload the next time Claude reads a matching file. If a rule must persist across compaction, drop the `paths:` frontmatter or move it to the project-root CLAUDE.md. Skill bodies are re-injected after compaction, but large skills are truncated to fit the per-skill cap, and the oldest invoked skills are dropped once the total budget is exceeded.\\nL94: Truncation keeps the start of the file, so put the most important instructions near the top of `SKILL.md`.\\nL95: ##\\nL96: \\nL97: cite39†​ L98: \\nL99: When your context fills up\\nL100: \\nL101: Claude Code compacts automatically as you approach the limit, so a full context window doesn’t end your session. The automatic pass works the same way as the `/compact` step in the timeline. See cite40†When context fills up for what it preserves. You can also act before the automatic pass runs:\\nL102:   * Compact with a focus: run `/compact` with instructions, like `/compact focus on the auth bug fix`, before starting a long new task. The summary keeps what you choose instead of what the automatic pass guesses is important.\\nL103:   * Compact earlier: run cite41†`/autocompact` with a token count, like `/autocompact 500k`, to set how full the context window gets before the automatic pass runs. See cite42†Set the auto-compact window for accepted values and overrides.\\nL104:   * Clear between tasks: run `/clear` when switching to unrelated work. Old conversation crowds out the files you need next and costs tokens on every message.\\nL105:   * Delegate large reads: send research to a cite36†subagent so the file contents stay in its context window, not yours.\\nL106: If you need a larger window rather than a smaller conversation, Fable 5, Sonnet 5, Opus 4.6 and later, and Sonnet 4.6 support a 1 million token context window. See cite43†Extended context for availability by plan and how to select a `[1m]` model variant. Sonnet 5 runs at 1M with no `[1m]` variant to select; see cite44†Sonnet 5 context window for its auto-compaction thresholds and the LLM gateway exception. Compaction works the same way at the larger limit.\\nL107: The point where automatic compaction runs depends on your model and configuration. See cite45†Default auto-compact thresholds for the boundaries per model, and cite46†Correct the window for a gateway or custom model ID if Claude Code assumes the wrong window for your model ID, such as an cite47†LLM gateway alias.\\nL108: ##\\nL109: \\nL110: cite48†​ L111: \\nL112: Check your own session\\nL113: \\nL114: The visualization uses representative numbers. To see your actual context usage at any point, run `/context` for a live breakdown by category with optimization suggestions, including which CLAUDE.md and auto memory files loaded. Run `/memory` to open and edit those files.\\nL115: ##\\nL116: \\nL117: cite49†​ L118: \\nL119: Related resources\\nL120: \\nL121: For deeper coverage of the features shown in the timeline, see these pages:\\nL122:   * cite13†Extend Claude Code : when to use CLAUDE.md vs skills vs rules vs hooks vs MCP\\nL123:   * cite17†Store instructions and memories : CLAUDE.md hierarchy and auto memory\\nL124:   * cite36†Subagents : delegate research to a separate context window\\nL125:   * cite22†Best practices : managing context as your primary constraint\\nL126:   * cite16†Prompt caching : which actions invalidate the cached prefix\\nL127:   * cite50†Reduce token usage : strategies for keeping context usage low\\nL128: \\nL129: Was this page helpful?\\nL130: \\nL131: Yes No\\nL132: cite14†Explore the .claude directory cite16†Prompt caching L133: \\nL134: cite2†Claude Code Docs home page L135: \\nL136: cite51†x†x.com cite52†linkedin†www.linkedin.com L137: \\nL138: Company\\nL139: \\nL140: cite53†Anthropic†www.anthropic.com cite54†Careers†www.anthropic.com cite55†Economic Futures†www.anthropic.com cite56†Research†www.anthropic.com cite57†News†www.anthropic.com cite58†Trust center†trust.anthropic.com cite59†Transparency†www.anthropic.com L141: \\nL142: Help and security\\nL143: \\nL144: cite60†Availability†www.anthropic.com cite61†Status†status.anthropic.com cite62†Support center†support.claude.com L145: \\nL146: Learn\\nL147: cite63†Courses†www.anthropic.com cite64†MCP connectors†claude.com cite65†Customer stories†www.claude.com cite66†Engineering blog†www.anthropic.com cite67†Events†www.anthropic.com cite68†Powered by Claude†claude.com cite69†Service partners†claude.com cite70†Startups program†claude.com L148: \\nL149: Terms and policies\\nL150: \\nL151: cite71†Privacy choices†www.anthropic.com cite71†Privacy policy†www.anthropic.com cite72†Disclosure policy†www.anthropic.com cite73†Usage policy†www.anthropic.com cite74†Commercial terms†www.anthropic.com cite75†Consumer terms