uses cite279†subagents to accelerate complex work, making it useful for larger tasks that can be split across subagents.\nL786: \nL787: 5.6 Sol Extra High Selections in this preview don't change your ChatGPT settings.5.6 Sol Extra High, 5 of 6.Use Left and Right arrow keys to adjust power.\nL788: ## Choose a model\nL789: \nL790: In an interactive CLI session, use `/model` to switch models or adjust reasoning effort. You can also choose a model when you launch Codex with `--model` or its `-m` alias:\nL791: \nL792:     `codex --model gpt-5.6`\nL793: \nL794: The same option works with non-interactive runs. For example:\nL795: \nL796:     `codex exec -m gpt-5.6 \"Review the current changes\"`\nL797: Higher reasoning effort can improve results for complex tasks, but it takes longer and uses more tokens. Start with the default effort and increase it when the task needs deeper planning or analysis.\nL798: \nL799: Ultra mode goes beyond a single-agent run. It uses cite279†subagents to accelerate complex work, making it useful for larger tasks that can be split across subagents.\nL800: \nL801: Interactive Codex CLI reasoning level selector\nL802: \nL803: Select Reasoning Level for gpt-5.6-sol\nL804: \nL805: 1.Low Fast responses with lighter reasoning\nL806: 2.Medium (default)Balances speed and reasoning depth for everyday tasks\nL807: \nL808: 3.High Greater reasoning depth for complex problems\nL809: \nL810: 4.Extra high Extra high reasoning depth for complex problems\nL811: \nL812: 5.Max Maximum reasoning depth for the hardest problems\nL813: \nL814: ›6.Ultra (current)Maximum reasoning with automatic task delegation\nL815: \nL816: Press enter to confirm or esc to go back\nL817: ## Choose a model\nL818: \nL819: Use the model switcher below the composer to choose an available model and reasoning effort.\nL820: \nL821: Higher reasoning effort can improve results for complex tasks, but it takes longer and uses more tokens. Start with the default effort and increase it when the task needs deeper planning or analysis.\nL822: \nL823: Ultra mode goes beyond a single-agent run. It uses cite279†subagents to accelerate complex work, making it useful for larger tasks that can be split across subagents.\nL824: 5.6 Sol Extra High Selections in this preview don't change your ChatGPT settings.5.6 Sol Extra High, 5 of 6.Use Left and Right arrow keys to adjust power.\nL825: ## Recommended models\nL826: \nL827: cite424†Image: 5.6 Sol L828: \nL829: 5.6 Sol\nL830: \nL831: Flagship GPT-5.6 model with the strongest capability for complex coding, computer use, research, and cybersecurity.\nL832: \nL833: codex -m gpt-5.6-sol\nL834: \nL835: Copy command\nL836: \nL837: Capability\nL838: \nL839: Speed\nL840: \nL841: ChatGPT desktop app\nL842: \nL843: ChatGPT web\nL844: \nL845: Codex CLI\nL846: \nL847: Codex IDE extension\nL848: \nL849: Codex cloud\nL850: \nL851: ChatGPT Credits\nL852: \nL853: API Access\nL854: \nL855: cite425†Image: 5.6 Terra L856: \nL857: 5.6 Terra\nL858: \nL859: Balanced GPT-5.6 model for everyday work, with performance competitive with GPT-5.5 at a lower cost.\nL860: \nL861: codex -m gpt-5.6-terra\nL862: \nL863: Copy command\nL864: Capability\nL865: \nL866: Speed\nL867: \nL868: ChatGPT desktop app\nL869: \nL870: ChatGPT web\nL871: \nL872: Codex CLI\nL873: \nL874: Codex IDE extension\nL875: \nL876: Codex cloud\nL877: \nL878: ChatGPT Credits\nL879: \nL880: API Access\nL881: \nL882: cite426†Image: 5.6 Luna L883: \nL884: 5.6 Luna\nL885: \nL886: Fast and affordable GPT-5.6 model that delivers strong capability at the lowest cost in the family.\nL887: \nL888: codex -m gpt-5.6-luna\nL889: \nL890: Copy command\nL891: \nL892: Capability\nL893: \nL894: Speed\nL895: \nL896: ChatGPT desktop app\nL897: \nL898: ChatGPT web\nL899: \nL900: Codex CLI\nL901: \nL902: Codex IDE extension\nL903: \nL904: Codex cloud\nL905: \nL906: ChatGPT Credits\nL907: \nL908: API Access\nL909: \nL910: cite427†Image: 5.3 Codex Spark L911: \nL912: 5.3 Codex Spark\nL913: Text-only research preview model optimized for near-instant, real-time coding iteration. Available to ChatGPT Pro users.\nL914: \nL915: codex -m gpt-5.3-codex-spark\nL916: \nL917: Copy command\nL918: \nL919: Capability\nL920: \nL921: Speed\nL922: \nL923: ChatGPT desktop app\nL924: \nL925: ChatGPT web\nL926: \nL927: Codex CLI\nL928: \nL929: Codex IDE extension\nL930: \nL931: Codex cloud\nL932: \nL933: ChatGPT Credits\nL934: \nL935: API Access\nL936: Start with the default Power setting, which uses `gpt-5.6-sol` with medium reasoning. Move toward Smarter for deeper reasoning or Faster for faster, lower-cost work. Open Advanced when you want `gpt-5.6-luna` or a specific model, reasoning effort, or speed.\nL937: ## Choosing Sol, Terra, and Luna\nL938: \nL939: Codex offers three GPT-5.6 models: Sol for detail and polish, Terra as the everyday workhorse, and Luna for clear, repeatable work. If you are unsure, start with Sol.\nL940: ### Where each model shines\nL941:   * Sol, for complex, open-ended work. Choose Sol for ambiguous, difficult, or high-value tasks that need extra analysis, judgment, or polish, such as complex code changes, deep research, or polished documents. For narrower tasks, define what done looks like to keep the work focused.\nL942:   * Terra, the pragmatic all-rounder. Choose Terra for everyday work that needs strong reasoning and tool use when you do not need Sol’s full depth. It is a natural starting point for work you previously gave GPT-5.5.\nL943:   * Luna, for clear, repeatable tasks. Choose Luna for specific, high-volume tasks when you know what a good result looks like, such as extraction, classification, transformation, and structured summaries.\nL944: ### Pick a reasoning effort\nL945: \nL946: Use the lowest reasoning effort that produces the result you need. Increase it for tasks that need more planning, analysis, or checking.\nL947: \nL948:   * Light in the ChatGPT desktop app, ChatGPT Work on the web, and IDE extension, or Low in the CLI, suits quick, well-scoped tasks.\nL949:   * Medium balances speed and depth for tasks that need more planning.\nL950:   * High and Extra High suit difficult work with multiple steps, sources, or tradeoffs.\nL951: There is no exact mapping from GPT-5.5 reasoning efforts to GPT-5.6. Try a familiar task at a lower setting and adjust based on the result.\nL952: ### Know when to use Max or Ultra\nL953: \nL954: Max gives the selected model more time to reason about a single task. Use it for the hardest problems, when depth matters more than speed or usage. If you don’t see Max in your options, you’ll have to enable it in your app settings.\nL955: \nL956: Ultra uses cite279†subagents to handle separate parts of a complex task in parallel. Choose it when you can divide the work into meaningful parts. Most tasks do not need Max or Ultra.\nL957: If Ultra doesn’t appear in the desktop app’s model slider, go to Settings > Configuration, then turn on Ultra in model picker slider.\nL958: ## Other models\nL959: \nL960: When you sign in with ChatGPT, Codex works best with the recommended models listed above.\nL961: \nL962: GPT-5.4 and GPT-5.4 mini retire from Codex on August 31, 2026.\nL963: \nL964: If you sign in with ChatGPT, replace `gpt-5.4` with `gpt-5.6-terra` and `gpt-5.4-mini` with `gpt-5.6-luna` in saved configurations, custom agents, and scheduled tasks. The OpenAI API and Codex authenticated with your own API key aren’t affected.\nL965: \nL966: View other models\nL967: \nL968: cite428†Image: 5.5 L969: \nL970: 5.5\nL971: Previous-generation frontier model for complex coding, computer use, knowledge work, and research workflows.\nL972: \nL973: codex -m gpt-5.5\nL974: \nL975: Copy command\nL976: \nL977: Capability\nL978: \nL979: Speed\nL980: \nL981: ChatGPT desktop app\nL982: \nL983: ChatGPT web\nL984: \nL985: Codex CLI\nL986: \nL987: Codex IDE extension\nL988: \nL989: Codex cloud\nL990: \nL991: ChatGPT Credits\nL992: \nL993: API Access\nL994: \nL995: cite429†Image: 5.4 L996: \nL997: 5.4\nL998: \nL999: Frontier model for professional work with strong coding, reasoning, tool use, and agentic workflow capabilities.\nL1000: \nL1001: codex -m gpt-5.4\nL1002: \nL1003: Copy command\nL1004: \nL1005: Capability\nL1006: \nL1007: Speed\nL1008: \nL1009: ChatGPT desktop app\nL1010: \nL1011: ChatGPT web\nL1012: \nL1013: Codex CLI\nL1014: Codex IDE extension\nL1015: \nL1016: Codex cloud\nL1017: \nL1018: ChatGPT Credits\nL1019: \nL1020: API Access\nL1021: \nL1022: cite430†Image: 5.4 Mini L1023: \nL1024: 5.4 Mini\nL1025: \nL1026: Fast, efficient mini model for responsive coding tasks and subagents.\nL1027: \nL1028: codex -m gpt-5.4-mini\nL1029: \nL1030: Copy command\nL1031: \nL1032: Capability\nL1033: \nL1034: Speed\nL1035: \nL1036: ChatGPT desktop app\nL1037: \nL1038: ChatGPT web\nL1039: \nL1040: Codex CLI\nL1041: \nL1042: Codex IDE extension\nL1043: \nL1044: Codex cloud\nL1045: \nL1046: ChatGPT Credits\nL1047: \nL1048: API Access\nL1049: You can also point Codex at any model and provider that supports either the cite431†Chat Completions†platform.openai.com or cite432†Responses APIs†platform.openai.com to fit your specific use case.\nL1050: \nL1051: Support for the Chat Completions API is deprecated and will be removed in future releases of Codex.\nL1052: ## Deprecated Codex models\nL1053: \nL1054: The `gpt-5.4` and `gpt-5.4-mini` models retire from Codex with ChatGPT sign-in on August 31, 2026. Replace `gpt-5.4` with `gpt-5.6-terra` and `gpt-5.4-mini` with `gpt-5.6-luna` in workspace defaults, saved model settings, managed configurations, custom agents, and scheduled tasks.\nL1055: The `gpt-5.2` and `gpt-5.3-codex` models are already deprecated in Codex when you sign in with ChatGPT. Update scripts, configuration files, and `codex exec --model` commands that still reference those models.\nL1056: \nL1057: The OpenAI API and Codex authenticated with your own API key aren’t affected by the GPT-5.4 retirement. For current A